      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">Can You Start in a Group and Move to Private Lessons?</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Yes, and plenty of adults do. Private and group learning aren't competing paths. They tend to work well together over time.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">A group class can be a gentle way through the very beginning: learning how to hold the instrument, making your first clear sounds, and building the habit of showing up each week. Once you know you want to keep going, private lessons can take what you've built and shape it around your own goals. It can work the other way too. Some adults start privately to get their setup solid, then enjoy playing with others later on.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">What matters is that you keep learning in a way that feels supportive, and that you can adjust as your goals become clearer.</p>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <h2 class="mt-12 font-heading font-bold text-ink" style="font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.025em;">A Simple Way to Decide</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">If you're still unsure, these questions usually point the way:</p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 pl-5 list-none">
        {[
          "Do I have a specific goal, or do I mostly want to find out whether I enjoy violin?",
          "Have I played before, and are my skills uneven in ways a group might not fit?",
          "How much individual feedback do I want while I'm building my foundations?",
          "Would other learners make me feel more relaxed, or more self-conscious?",
          "Can I keep a fixed weekly time, or do I need flexibility?",
        ].map(item => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-base text-ink/70 font-light leading-relaxed">
            <span class="mt-2 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-purplePrimary opacity-60 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            {item}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">If your answers lean toward specific goals, uneven experience, and wanting close feedback, private lessons are probably your starting point. If they lean toward exploring, wanting company, and liking a clear structure, a small group class may be the friendlier first step. And if you're somewhere in the middle, that's a good conversation to have with a teacher before you decide.</p>

      <!-- Mid-article CTA -->
